{"qid":"Q486181","names":["Samanid Empire"],"title":"Samanid Empire","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Samanid Empire was a Persianate Sunni Muslim dynasty of Iranian dehqan origin that ruled Transoxiana and Khorasan from its capital at Bukhara, attested in this dataset from 886 to 999. Founded by Ismail ibn Ahmad, who defeated the Saffarids in 900 to consolidate control over eastern Iran and Central Asia, the Samanids became the foremost patrons of New Persian literature and culture, fostering poets such as Rudaki and the early stages of Firdausi's work. Their bureaucratic and fiscal apparatus, modeled on Abbasid precedents, made Bukhara one of the Islamic world's great intellectual centers in the tenth century. The dynasty collapsed under pressure from the Ghaznavids in the southeast and the Qarakhanid Turks in the northeast, with the last Samanid claimant killed in 1005.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Samanid territorial extent is reasonably well-documented through administrative chronicles (Narshakhi's History of Bukhara, Ibn Hawqal's geography) and numismatic evidence, though precise frontier lines with the Qarakhanids and Buyids in border zones are conjectural.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Samanid dynasty","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Samanid-dynasty"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Samanid Empire","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samanid_Empire"}],"britannica":"topic/Samanid-dynasty","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Samanid Empire","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Saman Khuda","nativeName":"سامان خدا","roman":"Sāmān Khudā","start":819,"end":819,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLic